\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/figure_ladder.pdf}
\caption{The ladder. A task stops at the direct answer $L_0$ when it is confident, or, at the calibrated
depth, runs the single agent $L_1$ and stops when the two agree and $L_1$ is confident; otherwise it runs
the full pipeline from scratch.}
\label{fig:ladder}
\end{figure}

\climb{} solves the constrained problem~(\ref{eq:constrained}) in two steps. A structural step
(\S\ref{sec:structure}) shows that, under one condition on the gain, the optimal rule is a threshold on the
score; a statistical step (\S\ref{sec:calib}) selects the threshold by testing candidates in order, with a
guarantee that needs no condition. \S\ref{sec:ladder} instantiates the ladder and the score.

\subsection{When a threshold is optimal}
\label{sec:structure}

We first take the one-decision setting of \S\ref{sec:problem}. The condition is that escalation
buys less the more confident the cheap answer is.

\begin{itemize}
  \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
  \item[(M)] \emph{Monotone gain}: $g(s)$ is non-increasing in $s$, and so is $g(s)/c(s)$, the
  accuracy bought per token of escalation.
\end{itemize}

\noindent When the pipeline's cost varies little with the score within a task distribution, the
two statements nearly coincide.

\begin{proposition}[Threshold optimality]
\label{prop:threshold}
With one decision and under (M), for every $\eps > 0$ the constrained problem~(\ref{eq:constrained}) has an optimal
policy that is either $\varnothing$ or stops when $S > t$ and escalates when $S < t$, for some
threshold $t$. If $S$ has a continuous distribution, this policy is deterministic.
\end{proposition}

The constrained problem is a fractional knapsack whose items are score values, weighted by the risk
$g(s)$ of stopping there and valued by the cost $c(s)$ it saves; under (M) filling it greedily means
stopping above a threshold (Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}). Each family \climb{} certifies makes exactly one
decision, after $L_0$ or after both cheap rungs, so the proposition applies as stated.

\subsection{Risk-controlled calibration}
\label{sec:calib}

The optimal threshold depends on the unknown $g$ and $c$. Rather than estimating them, we select it
with a distribution-free procedure. Following Learn-then-Test \citep{angelopoulos2021ltt}, each
candidate threshold becomes a null hypothesis that its risk exceeds the tolerance, and a threshold
is deployable only if the calibration data reject that hypothesis.

\paragraph{Candidates.} $\Lambda = (\varnothing, t_1, \dots, t_M)$ with $t_1 > \dots > t_M$, fixed
before any calibration label is seen and ending at $t_M = -\infty$, which never escalates. Lower thresholds stop more tasks, so the sequence runs from conservative to aggressive and
from expensive to cheap. The action $\varnothing$ has zero risk, so it is always admissible.

\paragraph{Testing the loss itself.} The per-task loss of threshold $t$,
$\ell = Y_{\mathrm{ref}} - Y_{\pi_t} \in \{-1, 0, 1\}$, is signed: stopping can lose an
answer the pipeline gets right, or keep one the pipeline would have revised into a wrong answer.
We test its mean directly with a betting test \citep{waudbysmith2024betting}. Under
$\mathcal{H}_m: \E[\ell_{t_m}] > \eps$, the wealth of a gambler who bets against the loss exceeding
$\eps$,
\begin{equation}
  W_j = \prod_{i \le j} \big(1 + \lambda_i (\eps - \ell_i)\big), \qquad 0 \le \lambda_i < \tfrac{1}{1 - \eps},
\end{equation}
is a nonnegative supermartingale whenever each $\lambda_i$ depends only on earlier tasks, so by
Ville's inequality \citep{ville1939} $p_m = 1 / \max_j W_j$ is a valid p-value. We set $\lambda_i$
from the running variance of the earlier losses, which makes the test adapt to how often stopping
changes the answer at all.

\paragraph{Sequential testing.} On $n$ calibration tasks, each with every cheap rung's and the
reference's outcomes recorded, we test $\mathcal{H}_1, \mathcal{H}_2, \dots$ in order and stop at the
first $m$ with $p_m > \delta$. The admissible set $\mathcal{C}$ contains $\varnothing$ and every
rejected threshold, and \climb{} deploys its cheapest member (Algorithm~\ref{alg:climb} in
Appendix~\ref{app:impl}).

\paragraph{Choosing the depth.} Whether the second rung pays for itself depends on the model and the
task: when $L_0$ is usually right, every escalated task would pay for $L_1$ in addition to the
pipeline. \climb{} therefore lets the calibration data choose the depth. It runs the test above on two
one-decision families, a threshold on $L_0$'s score ($D = 0$) and, after both cheap rungs, a threshold on
$S_1$, which combines the two rungs' agreement with $L_1$'s confidence ($D = 1$), each at level $\delta/2$, and deploys the cheaper of the two certified policies. By a union bound both
certificates hold at once with probability at least $1 - \delta$, so the choice costs nothing in
validity.

\begin{proposition}[Risk control]
\label{prop:risk}
If calibration and deployment tasks, together with the model's outputs on them, are i.i.d., then
with probability at least $1 - \delta$ every policy in $\mathcal{C}_0 \cup \mathcal{C}_1$, including the deployed one,
has $R \le \eps$. The deployed policy's mean calibration cost is at most the pipeline's.
\end{proposition}

Testing in a fixed order needs no correction for the $M$ candidates, because a true null can be rejected
only if the first true null in the sequence is, which happens with probability at most the level; on the event that none is, every certified
policy is safe, so deploying the cheapest costs nothing (Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}). Whether the procedure
also \emph{finds} the cheap policies is what the structural condition answers.

\begin{proposition}[Efficiency]
\label{prop:efficiency}
Let $r(t)$ and $J(t)$ be the risk and expected cost of the threshold $t$. With one decision and under (M):
(i) the thresholds with $r(t) \le \eps$ form a prefix $t_1, \dots, t_{m_\eps}$ of the sequence;
(ii) $J$ decreases along the sequence, so $t_{m_\eps}$ is the cheapest safe threshold;
(iii) for every $\eta > 0$, the threshold \climb{} deploys has expected cost at most
$J(t_{m_{\eps-\eta}}) + o_P(1)$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{proposition}

Part (i) is what makes testing in order lose nothing: stopping at the first failure discards no safe
threshold. Under (M) lowering the threshold adds tasks of larger gain, so the risk is convex in the share
stopped and starts at zero. With Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold}, (iii) says that \climb{}'s cost approaches
that of the best one-decision policy on the same score as calibration data grow (Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}).

\paragraph{Scope of the guarantee.} Proposition~\ref{prop:risk} needs neither (M) nor a calibrated score:
a weak or even misleading signal tends to cost savings rather than accuracy beyond the tolerance. It bounds the loss
relative to the pipeline, not to the best achievable system, and its i.i.d.\ assumption covers the
model's outputs as well as the tasks. We set $\delta = 0.1$; the threshold grid is given in Appendix~\ref{app:impl}.

\subsection{Instantiation}
\label{sec:ladder}

\paragraph{The ladder.} Two cheap rungs use the same model as the pipeline. $L_0$ answers directly at
the model's cheapest reasoning setting (low effort, or thinking off where the model has no graded
setting). $L_1$ is a single agent at the pipeline's own reasoning setting: it answers the question from
scratch, without $L_0$'s answer, exactly as the pipeline's executor would without a plan or a review.
Every call ends with a line \texttt{ANSWER: <answer>} and may emit Python blocks, which are executed
and their output returned, up to three times before a closing call asks for the answer.

\paragraph{The pipeline.} An escalated task runs the reference from scratch: a planner writes a
step-by-step plan, the executor answers given the plan, and a reviewer judges the answer, ending with
a line \texttt{VERDICT: YES} or \texttt{NO}; a rejection prompts a revision, for at most five rounds. A
review whose reasoning fills the call cap gets one closing call that asks for the verdict alone. Its context is rebuilt each round from the question,
plan and only the latest answer and review. Tasks run to completion, with no per-task token budget, so
the harms we measure are not artefacts of truncation; generation caps
per call are given in Appendix~\ref{app:details}.

\paragraph{The score.} $S_0$ is the mean token log-probability of the call that produced $L_0$'s answer,
which the inference server returns at no extra cost. After both rungs, the score adds whether they agree:
$S_1 = 10 \cdot \mathbf{1}[a_0 = a_1] + \bar s_1$, where $a_k$ is rung $k$'s normalised answer and $\bar s_1$
the mean log-probability of $L_1$'s final call. Since $L_1$ answers without seeing $L_0$'s answer, agreement
between them is evidence that costs no extra tokens. As $-10 < \bar s_1 \le 0$ on all our records, every task on which the two rungs
agree ranks above every task on which they do not, and the threshold sequence first stops agreeing tasks
in order of $L_1$'s confidence.
